  "behavior_evidence": [
    "Nine native rows cover default empty, reserved empty, single/multiple values, retained empty storage, assignment over live values from all storage states, and self assignment. Container allocation/payload identity, capacity, source nullness and destructor event order match.",
    "Native receiver destructors assert that the actual source pointer, size and capacity remain unchanged until they all finish. The Rust implementation now calls clear(true) before from_moved(source).",
    "The existing original MoveConstructor declaration previously called a move-assignment helper; it now calls from_moved with the same original inputs and assertions. The MoveAssignment case retains its own operation.",
    "A destructor-panic probe on baseline empties source to 0/0 and installs its 2/4 allocation in the receiver despite failure. Candidate retains the source at 2/4 with the original pointer/payload and empties the receiver; no source element is destroyed.",
    "MoveItem has neither Clone nor Default, and transfer tests preserve boxed payload addresses. Borrowed lifetimes, covariance, Send/Sync/Unpin and unwind auto-traits remain valid."
  ],
  "remaining_callable_work": [
    "LinearVector Erase still uses Vec removal rather than native move-assignment callbacks, and Insert still lacks native move-construction/destruction of each shifted slot. These remain the next concrete method gaps.",
    "Automatic C++ element trait dispatch for growth remains an explicit relocation callback policy in Rust. No element callback machinery was changed here."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Native move assignment is noexcept: an element destructor exception terminates. Rust panic recovery is documented/tested as a safety extension, not successful native exception equivalence.",
    "The common domain is non-zero-sized Rust-valid payloads and non-panicking native destruction; source/receiver references cannot alias in safe Rust, so native self assignment uses assign_self().",
    "Native destructor observation of source metadata is proven by the native fixture and production call order; no unsafe shared Rust alias is introduced to inspect a container under exclusive borrowing.",
    "Standard allocator identity/hooks, ABI layouts, zero-sized element differences, and writable native const Array1 aliases remain outside this receipt.",
    "No global API/test ledger was changed; exact method evidence does not establish whole-class or kernel parity."
  ],
